{"step_index":297,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-13T05:24:03Z","input_tokens":3771,"cache_read_tokens":175028,"output_tokens":2791,"thinking":"Examining the palette component in `BlockEditor.tsx`, specifically the button rendering logic within the map function, reveals a potential area for user confusion with the \"move\" action. The text displayed on the button is dynamically generated. The hypothesis is that the dynamic text generation may not clearly communicate the direction of the \"move\" action, contributing to user misunderstanding at Level 3.\n\n\nThe button text for \"put\" actions is not specific, displaying only \"Masukkan\" regardless of the target box. This lack of differentiation creates a usability issue because the user cannot distinguish between different \"put\" actions in the palette. Upon inspection of the `fromPalette` and `getBlockLabel` functions, it confirms the missing context on the button itself is added when rendering the block in the program, not on the initial palette button. This means the user has to guess what will happen when a \"put\" action is added.\n\n\nThe \"put\" action in the palette immediately adds blocks to the root program, bypassing conditional logic within \"then\" or \"else\" blocks.  This behavior, combined with the lack of specific context in the \"Masukkan\" label, leads to ambiguity about where the \"put\" action will place the target.  Nested block placement via drag-and-drop on mobile could also be difficult. Multiple \"if\" blocks in the palette add further confusion, as buttons just display \"Jika...\" without condition context.\n\n\nThe `SortView` component in `BoardView.tsx` visualizes a sorting process, displaying items transitioning between an item queue and boxes.  The code uses `frames` and `currentIdx` to approximate processing and completion status of items within the sorting sequence.\n\n\nThe initial state of `SortView` shows no active sorting, as the `frames` array is empty. The user interface displays a sequence of items, but the visual feedback doesn't explain the sorting process initially. It's unclear how the displayed emojis represent a sorting activity without any movement or indication of sorting logic. The user needs a clear visual cue to understand what the task is.\n\n\nThe sorting process in `SortView` lacks clear visual representation of the conditional \"if...then...else\" logic. The existing animation only shows items disappearing and reappearing in boxes, omitting the crucial intermediate step of evaluation.  The code doesn't depict the \"if\" condition being checked, creating a misleading visual. The current implementation of the animation relies on a broken processing state and jumps directly from the input queue to sorted boxes. Further, the block editor allows placement of the \"put\" action outside of the conditional statement.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"e:\\\\codekids\\\\src\\\\lib\\\\missions\\\\engine.ts\"","EndLine":"250","StartLine":"170","toolAction":"\"Viewing sort engine in engine.ts\"","toolSummary":"\"View sort engine in engine.ts\""}}]}
